  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 AM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nLate tonight, a fairly disorganized area of showers and isolated \nthunderstorms were moving east along the I-70 corridor, tied to a \ncold frontal zone. The disorganization is largely due to lack of \nappreciable moisture and instability (MUCAPE 500 J/kg or less). This \nwill be a continuing theme along this frontal zone as it continues \nto move slowly south across southwest Kansas on Tuesday then again \nWednesday. For this reason, there is a very low probability of \nsevere storms with this front, and even the chances of widespread \nrainfall amounts of 1/4\" or higher are dwindling in the forecast. \nLow level moisture transport will favor central and south central \nKansas, perhaps including our eastern counties, generally along/east \nof Highway 183 later Wednesday and Thursday Night. \n\nThe cold front later on today will will provide a nice respite from \nthe heat, if only for a couple of days. Afternoon temperatures today \nwill likely track closer to the NBM 25th percentile given the fairly \nstrong north-northeast winds and some increased cloud cover (highs \nof around 80 at Scott City to WaKeeney). Along and south of the slow-\nmoving front, upper 90s to near 100 are likely once again, generally \nsoutheast of a Meade to Larned line -- especially down in the \ntypically hot Red Hills region.\n\nThe frontal zone will likely be along the Oklahoma line late \nWednesday and lift slowly back north as a warm front Thursday, and \nas long as the front remains around our southwest Kansas region, the \ntemperature forecasting will continue to be a challenge (mainly \nafternoon temperatures). NBM ranges 82 WaKeeney to 101 Medicine \nLodge Wednesday with upper 90s/near 100 advancing slowly north into \nfar southwest Kansas Thursday as the frontal zone lifts back north. \nIf the front bisects southwest Kansas from west-northwest to east-\nsoutheast, we will likely see strong to marginally severe \nthunderstorms develop. Thursday late afternoon/evening will likely \nbe the best chance for the most organized thunderstorm activity -- \nagain mainly eastern areas where dewpoints will be higher closer to \ncentral and south central Kansas.\n\nThis weekend into much of next week, the summer subtropical high \nwill expand across the Rockies and central High Plains again, \nfavoring another 3+ day heat wave beginning as early as Friday in \nthe southwest.  Latest official forecast has at least three-quarters \nof the DDC CWA 100+ highs beginning Sunday, continuing through \nTuesday. Upper end high temperature potential (90th percentile NBM) \nshows some 105-106F highs across portions of mainly far southwest \nKansas and also up toward Hays Sunday through Tuesday of next\nweek.",
